Amazon RDS:
- Managed relational database (SQL).
- Multi-AZ = high availability and automatic failover.
- Read replicas = scale read traffic.
- Automated backups and point-in-time recovery.
- Security: IAM authentication, Secrets Manager, security groups, KMS encryption.

Amazon Aurora:
- AWS-optimised relational database (MySQL/PostgreSQL compatible).
- High availability and automatic failover.
- Automatic storage scaling.
- Reader and writer endpoints.
- Global Database = cross-region replication.
- Serverless = automatic capacity scaling.
- Machine learning integration.
- Database cloning.

ElastiCache:
- Managed in-memory caching (Redis OSS/Valkey or Memcached, depending on offering).
- Improves application performance and reduces database load.
- Redis OSS/Valkey = advanced data structures, replication and persistence options.
- Memcached = simple distributed caching.
- Use caching for frequently accessed data.

DynamoDB:
- Fully managed NoSQL key-value/document database.
- Serverless scaling and single-digit millisecond performance; microsecond latency with DAX for cached reads.
- On-demand or provisioned capacity.
- Global Tables = multi-region, multi-active replication.
- DynamoDB Streams = capture item-level changes.
- TTL = automatically expire items.
- DAX = in-memory read cache.
- Backups and point-in-time recovery.
- Security: IAM and encryption at rest.

  Amazon S3:
- Object storage for files, images, videos, backups and data lakes (not a traditional database).
- Buckets contain objects; each object has a key.
- Storage classes: Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, Standard-IA, One Zone-IA, Glacier Instant Retrieval, Glacier Flexible Retrieval, Deep Archive.
- Versioning = recover previous object versions.
- Lifecycle policies = automatically transition or expire objects.
- Replication = copy objects across buckets/regions (CRR/SRR).
- Encryption: SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, SSE-C.
- Security: IAM policies, bucket policies, Block Public Access.
- Presigned URLs = temporary access to objects.
- Transfer Acceleration = faster long-distance uploads.
- S3 Object Lock = WORM protection against deletion/modification.
- S3 Select = retrieve subsets of data from supported objects.
- Event Notifications = trigger Lambda, SQS or SNS.
- Strong read-after-write consistency.

## Amazon Neptune
- Fully managed graph database for highly connected data (e.g. social networks, fraud detection, recommendations).
- Graph models: represent data as nodes and relationships (edges).
- Query languages: Gremlin and openCypher for property graphs; SPARQL for RDF graphs.
- Neptune Streams = capture changes to graph data via an HTTP REST API.
- High availability through replication across multiple Availability Zones.
- Exam tip: Use Neptune when relationships between data points are central to the application.

## Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra)
- Fully managed, serverless, Apache Cassandra-compatible wide-column NoSQL database.
- Supports Cassandra Query Language (CQL).
- Automatically scales storage; supports on-demand and provisioned capacity modes.
- Data is replicated across multiple Availability Zones for high availability.
- Encryption at rest and in transit.
- Exam tip: Use Keyspaces when you need Cassandra compatibility without managing database servers.

## Amazon Timestream
- Fully managed time-series database for time-stamped data (e.g. IoT, application metrics, sensor data).
- Automatically scales to handle large volumes of time-series data.
- Purpose-built for fast time-based queries and time-series analytics.
- Memory Store = recent data for fast access.
- Magnetic Store = older data for cost-effective storage.
- Scheduled queries = prepare and aggregate data for faster analysis.
- Encryption at rest and in transit.
- Exam tip: Use Timestream for time-stamped measurements and events that must be queried over time.
  
  
